L62: # Risk-sensitive reinforcement learning using expectiles, shortfall risk and optimized certainty equivalent risk
L63: Sumedh Gupte^{*} Affiliation: Indian Institute of Technology Madras Email: cs21d014@smail.iitm.ac.in    Shrey Rakeshkumar Patel ^{*} Affiliation: Indian Institute of Technology Madras Email: me21b138@smail.iitm.ac.in    Soumen Pachal Affiliation: Indian Institute of Technology Madras Email: cs22d009@smail.iitm.ac.in    Prashanth L. A Affiliation: Indian Institute of Technology Madras Email: prashla@cse.iitm.ac.in    Sanjay P. Bhat Affiliation: TCS Research, India Email: sanjay.bhat@tcs.com
L64: ###### Abstract
L65: We propose risk-sensitive reinforcement learning algorithms catering to three families of risk measures, namely expectiles, utility-based shortfall risk and optimized certainty equivalent risk. For each risk measure, in the context of a finite horizon Markov decision process, we first derive a policy gradient theorem.
L66: Second, we propose estimators of the risk-sensitive policy gradient for each of the aforementioned risk measures, and establish $O\left(1/m\right)$ mean-squared error bounds for our estimators, where $m$ is the number of trajectories. Further, under standard assumptions for policy gradient-type algorithms, we establish smoothness of the risk-sensitive objective, in turn leading to stationary convergence rate bounds for the overall risk-sensitive policy gradient algorithm that we propose.
L67: Finally, we conduct numerical experiments to validate the theoretical findings on popular RL benchmarks.
L68: ^{*}^{*}footnotetext: Equal contribution
L69: ## 1 Introduction
L70: In recent years, risk-sensitive reinforcement learning (RL) has received increased research attention, cf. [cite50†5 , cite51†21 , cite52†26 , cite53†22 , cite54†6 , cite55†28 , cite56†23 , cite57†33 ] for some representative works. The shortcomings of expected value as an objective for decision making and the need for incorporating risk is well-known, especially in domains such as finance, robotics, and transportation.
L71: Several risk measures have been studied in an RL framework, e.g., variance, quantiles, Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR), and cumulative prospect theory.
L72: In this paper, we consider three risk measures, namely expectiles [cite58†24 , cite59†3 ], utility-based shortfall risk (UBSR) [cite60†12 ] and optimized certainty equivalent (OCE) [cite61†4 ] risk. Each of these risk measures comes under the realm of convex risk measures, which in financial parlance ensures that diversification does not increase risk.
L73: UBSR and OCE constitute a rich class of risk measures with several interesting special cases, namely entropic risk, quadratic, CVaR, optimal net-present value (ONPV) [cite62†25 ], mean-variance and quartic risks [cite63†17 ], see cite64†Table 2 in Appendix cite21†A . For a textbook treatment of risk measures and their properties that capture the attitude towards risk, the reader is referred to [cite65†13 ].
L74: A prominent solution approach for optimizing risk measures in an RL context involves the use of policy gradient algorithms, see [cite66†29 ]. We now summarize our contributions for each class of risk measures that we study.
L75: Expectiles. First, we derive mean-squared error and high probability bounds for the classic estimator of expectiles from independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) samples. Second, we derive both lower and upper bounds for estimating an expectile of the cumulative discounted cost in a Markov cost process. Third, we derive an analogue of the policy gradient theorem albeit with an expectile objective for a finite-horizon MDP.
L76: Using this theorem, we form an estimator of the expectile gradient using sample trajectories of the underlying MDP and establish mean-squared error bounds for our proposed estimator.
L77: UBSR and OCE. Unlike expectiles, estimation of UBSR and OCE from i.i.d. samples has been studied in the literature, see [cite67†18 , cite63†17 ]. In this work, we focus on the policy gradient approach for UBSR and OCE. Towards this goal, we first derive policy gradient theorem analogues for UBSR and OCE objectives in a finite horizon MDP. This result is then employed to arrive at UBSR and OCE gradient estimators using trajectories simulated from the underlying MDP.
L78: Risk-aware policy gradient. We analyze a general policy gradient algorithm for optimizing a risk measure under the following assumptions: (i) the objective is smooth; and (ii) the MSE of gradient estimators is bounded above by $O(1/m)$, where $m$ is the number of trajectories. We establish convergence of this template algorithm to an approximate first-order stationary point in the non-asymptotic regime.
L79: Next, we show that expectiles, UBSR and OCE satisfy the aforementioned assumptions, and hence, the bounds for the template algorithm can be specialized to these risk measures.
L80: Experiments. We implemented REINFORCE [cite68†37 ] and our risk-aware policy gradient algorithms on Reacher MuJoCo environment. From our experiments, we observe that expectile and UBSR with a quadratic loss function outperform REINFORCE by exhibiting better average rewards and lower variance.
L81: Related work. Expectiles have been studied for several decades, see [cite69†34 , cite58†24 , cite59†3 , cite70†8 , cite71†2 , cite72†39 , cite73†1 ]. However, to the best of our knowledge, bounds for expectile estimation in the i.i.d. and Markovian cases have not been derived in the literature and our work fills this research gap. Moreover, in the RL framework, there is limited exploration of expectiles barring a few exceptions, see [cite74†7 , cite75†30 , cite76†20 ].
L82: In [cite74†7 ], the authors formulate a expectile variant of the Bellman equation and derive algorithms for finding a policy that solves this equation. In [cite75†30 , cite76†20 ], distributional RL is studied with expectiles. The aforementioned references do not consider a policy gradient approach for optimizing expectiles and the policy obtained by their algorithms do not necessarily optimize the expectile of the cumulative cost random variable.
L83: While UBSR and OCE estimation has been explored well in the literature, see [cite77†11 , cite78†10 , cite79†27 , cite63†17 , cite80†19 , cite67†18 ], there is limited research that integrates UBSR and OCE within a RL framework with the exception of [cite81†32 , cite82†38 ]. In [cite81†32 ], the authors study a nested variant of UBSR in a discounted MDP. As in the case of expectiles, the policy that optimizes nested UBSR is not necessarily optimal for UBSR applied directly to the cumulative cost random variable.
L84: We optimize the latter objective using policy gradients. In [cite82†38 ], the authors consider iterative algorithms for the episodic risk-sensitive RL problems using the OCE risk measure, and obtain regret bounds in a model-based setting. In contrast, we consider the model free setting, and derive a policy gradient theorem for OCE, and obtain non-asymptotic bounds on the convergence of the policy parameters.
L85: ## 2 Preliminaries
L86: We define a finite-horizon Markov Decision Process (MDP) as the tuple $\left<S,A,P,C,\gamma\right>$, where $S$ and $A$ denote the state space and the action space, respectively. We use the symbol $\Delta$ to denote a probability distribution. For instance, given a set $A$, $\Delta(A)$ denotes a probability distribution over $A$. The sets $S$ and $A$ are compact, and horizon length is given by $T$.
L87: The MDP transition kernel is defined as $P:S\times A\to\Delta(S)$, where $P(s^{\prime}|s,a)$ denotes the probability of going to state $s^{\prime}$ after taking action $a$ in state $s$ and $\Delta(S)$ denotes a probability distribution over $S$.
L88: The MDP cost function is defined as $C:S\times A\times S\to\Delta(\mathrm{R})$ respectively, and $\gamma\in[0,1]$ denotes the discount factor. The initial state distribution is denoted by $P_{0}$.
L89: The trajectory space is denoted by $\mathcal{T}$, where every $\tau\in\mathcal{T}$ is a set of states, actions and rewards, denoted as $\tau=\left<s_{0},a_{0},r_{1},s_{1},a_{1},r_{2},s_{2},\ldots,s_{T-1},a_{T-1},r_{T},s_{T}\right>$. The cumulative discounted cost $c(\tau)$ corresponding to trajectory $\tau$ is given by $c(\tau)=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^{t}C(s_{t},a_{t},s_{t+1})$.
L90: We consider the set of stochastic policies denoted as $\Pi$, where each $\pi:S\to\Delta(A)$ in $\Pi$ is Markovian and stationary. We assume that the policy set $\Pi$ is parameterized by $\Theta\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{d}$, a convex and compact subset in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$. Let $\theta\in\Theta$. Then $\pi_{\theta}:S\to\Delta(A)$ denotes a policy parameterized by $\theta$, and $p_{\theta}(\tau)$ denotes the probability of observing the trajectory $\tau$ under policy $\pi_{\theta}$.
L91: We define the score function $g:\Theta\times\mathcal{T}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}$ as
L92:  | $$g(\theta,\tau)=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\nabla_{\theta}\log\left(\pi_{\theta}\left(a_{t}|s_{t}\right)\right).$$  |  | (1)
L93: Let $(\mathcal{T},\Sigma)$ be a measurable space where $\Sigma$ is a $\sigma$-algebra on $\mathcal{T}$. The cumulative discounted cost of the MDP is given by the random variable $F$, defined on $(\mathcal{T},\Sigma)$. Each policy $\pi_{\theta}$ induces the measure $p_{\theta}$ on $F$, and $F$ takes the value $c(\tau)$ with probability $p_{\theta}(\tau)$.
L94: Throughout this paper, $G(\theta)$ denotes a random vector on $(\mathcal{T},\Sigma)$ that takes the value $g(\theta,\tau)$ with probability $p_{\theta}(\tau)$. Observe that $\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[G(\theta)\right]=0$.
L95: Risk-neutral objective. The expected cumulative discounted-cost associated with a policy $\pi_{\theta}$ is denoted by $J(\theta)$, where $J(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{\theta}\left[F\right]=\int_{\mathcal{T}}c(\tau)p_{\theta}(d\tau)$. Since the parameter $\theta$ affects the probability measure, we denote the expectation as $\mathbb{E}_{\theta}$. The risk-neutral RL objective is given by
L96: 
L97:  |  | $\displaystyle\text{find }\;\mathop{\rm arg\,min}_{\theta\in\Theta}J(\theta).$  |
L98: Risk-sensitive objective. We aim to find policies that are risk-optimal w.r.t. some desirable risk metric. Let $\mathit{L}_{0}$ denote the class of bounded and real-valued random variables. For every $\theta\in\Theta$, let $\rho_{\theta}:\mathit{L}_{0}\to\mathrm{R}$ denote a risk measure under probability measure $p_{\theta}$, that assigns a real value to a random variable in $\mathit{L}_{0}$. Then, the associated risk-sensitive objective is to find a $\theta^{*}$ such that
L99:  |  | $\displaystyle\theta^{*}\in\;\mathop{\rm arg\,min}_{\theta\in\Theta}h(\theta),\;\text{where}\;h(\theta)=\rho_{\theta}\left[F\right].$  |  | (2)
L100: 
L101: In this work, we shall consider the cases when $h$ is either the expectile risk, UBSR, or the OCE risk. We shall describe each of these risk measures in detail in the next section.
L102: ## 3 Risk measures
L103: 
L104: Throughout this paper, $X$ is a random variable that denotes costs, i.e., lower values are preferable. For instance, in a financial application, $X$ could be the loss associated with a financial position.
L105: 
L106: Expectiles. For a given $\nu\in(0,1)$, the expectile $\xi_{\nu}$ of $X$ is defined as follows:
L107: 
L108:  | $$\xi_{\nu}=\mathop{\rm arg\,min}_{k}\mathcal{L}_{\nu}(k),\textrm{ where }\mathcal{L}_{\nu}(k)\!=\!\mathbb{E}\left[e_{\nu}(X-k)\right],$$  |  | (3)
L109: with $e_{\nu}(x)=x^{2}\left|\nu-\mathbb{I}\left\{x\leq 0\right\}\right|$. Here $\mathbb{I}\left\{\cdot\right\}$ denotes the indicator function. For $\nu=0.5$, expectile coincides with the mean. In risk-sensitive optimization, $\nu>0.5$ corresponds to a risk-averse objective, while $\nu<0.5$ implies risk-seeking. Further, the loss function $\mathcal{L}_{\nu}(k)$ used to define the expectile is continuous and differentiable on $\mathbb{R}$.
L110: Utility-based shortfall risk. We define the UBSR using two parameters: a loss function $l$ and risk threshold $\lambda$. The loss function $l$ characterizes how the decision-maker perceives the costs $X$. Formally,
L111: ###### Definition 1.
L112: 
L113: The risk measure UBSR of a random variable $X$ for the loss function $l$ and risk threshold $\lambda$, is given by
L114: 
L115:  | $$\SRD_{l,\lambda}(X)\triangleq\inf\{\;k\in\mathbb{R}\;|\;\mathbb{E}[l(X-k)]\leq\lambda\}.$$  |  | (4)
L116: 
L117: Intuitively, the UBSR of cost $X$ is equal to the smallest quantity $k$ which when deducted from $X$, makes the expected loss $\mathbb{E}[l\left(X-k\right)]$ fall within the acceptable threshold $\lambda$.
L118: Popular examples of UBSR correspond to the choices $l(x)=e^{\beta x}$ for some $\beta>0$, and $l(x)=[x^{+}]^{2}$, where $x^{+}=\max(x,0)$. UBSR with $\lambda=1$ under the first choice is the well-known entropic risk measure.
L119: 
L120: Optimized certainty equivalent risk. The OCE risk is parameterized using a convex and increasing loss function $l$. Formally, we defined the OCE as
L121: ###### Definition 2.
L122: 
L123: Let the loss function $l:\mathbb{R}\to\mathbb{R}$ be a convex and increasing, and there exists $x\in\mathbb{R}$ such that $1\in\partial f(x)$. Then the OCE risk of random variable $X$ for a given loss function $l$ is defined as follows.
L124: 
L125:  | $$\OCD_{l}(X)\triangleq\inf_{k\in\mathbb{R}}\{k+\mathbb{E}\left[l(X-k)\right]\}.$$  |  | (5)
L126: Intuitively, the OCE risk optimizes over all possible splits of $X$ into $k$ and $X-k$. Precisely, the OCE of $X$ is equal to the minimum total cost (sum of current cost $k$ and future cost $l(X-k)$) where the minimum is over $k$.
L127: 
L128: The reader is referred to cite64†Table 2 in Appendix cite21†A for several risk measures that are special cases of UBSR and OCE risks.
L129: ## 4 Risk-sensitive RL with expectiles
L130: 
L131: Recall that an expectile at level $\nu$ is the minimizer of $\mathcal{L}_{\nu}(k)=\mathbb{E}[e_{\nu}(X-k)]$. It can be shown that $\mathcal{L}_{\nu}(k)$ is a strongly convex and smooth function of $k$, with respective parameters
L132: 
L133:  | $$\mu_{\nu}=2\min(\nu,1-\nu),\textrm{ and }L_{\nu}=2\max(\nu,1-\nu);$$  |  | (6)
L134: 
L135: for the proof of this claim, refer to the cite23†Section B.1 . Consequently, the expectile $\xi_{\nu}$ exists and is unique.
L136: The expectile $\xi_{\nu}$ can alternatively be characterized as the unique solution to the following (see Theorem 1 of [cite58†24 ]):
L137: 
L138:  | $$\mathbb{E}[l_{\nu}(X-\xi_{\nu})]=0,$$  |  | (7)
L139: 
L140: where $l_{\nu}(x)=\nu x\mathbb{I}\left\{x>0\right\}+(1-\nu)x\mathbb{I}\left\{x\leq 0\right\}$.
L141: ### 4.1 Estimation of expectiles
L142: 
L143: The i.i.d. case. Let $X_{1},X_{2},\dots,X_{m}$ be i.i.d. copies of a random variable $X$ with distribution function $H$. The empirical $\nu$-expectile $\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}$ is given by
L144: 
L145:  | $$\frac{1}{m}\sum_{i=1}^{m}l_{\nu}(X_{i}-\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m})=0.$$  |  | (8)
L146: 
L147: We next present a non-asymptotic bounds on the expectile estimate defined above.
L148: ###### Theorem 1.
L149: 
L150: Suppose $\mathbb{P}\left(X=\xi_{\nu}\right)=0$ and $X$ has a finite second moment. Then, we have the following bound for $\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}$ formed using (cite83†8 ):
L151: 
L152:  | $$\mathbb{E}[(\xi_{\nu}-\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m})^{2}]\leq\frac{L_{\nu}^{2}}{m\mu_{\nu}^{2}}\mathbb{E}[(X-\xi_{\nu})^{2}],$$  |  | (9)
L153: In addition, if $X$ is sub-Gaussian^{*}^{*} * A random variable $X$ is $\sigma$-sub-Gaussian if $\mathbb{E}[\exp(\lambda(X-\mathbb{E}[X]))]\leq\exp(\frac{\lambda^{2}\sigma^{2}}{2})$ for all $\lambda\in\mathbb{R}$. with parameter $\sigma$, then
L154: 
L155:  | $$\mathbb{P}\left[|\xi_{\nu}-\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}|\geq\epsilon\right]\leq 2\exp{\left(-\frac{m\epsilon^{2}\mu_{\nu}^{2}}{8L_{\nu}^{2}\sigma^{2}}\right)},$$  |  | (10)
L156: 
L157: where $\mu_{\nu},L_{\nu}$ are given in (cite84†6 ).
L158: The Markov case. Following the formulation in [cite85†35 ], we consider a Markov cost process (MCP), which is a tuple $(M,C)$. Here $M\equiv(\mathcal{S},P,\upsilon)$ is a Markov chain and $C$ is a cost function. The cumulative discounted cost over a $T$-length horizon is the random variable:
L159: 
L160:  | $$Z=\sum_{t=0}^{T-1}\gamma^{t}C_{t},\quad\text{where }C_{t}\sim C(s_{t})\text{ and }\gamma\in[0,1).$$  |  | (11)
L161: Let $\mathcal{H}$ denote the class of MCPs with horizon $T$. Let $\xi_{\nu}(M,C)$ denote the expectile of the cumulative discounted cost $Z$ at level $\nu\in(0,1)$. We are interested in estimating this quantity given a sample path of the underlying Markov chain.
L162: Estimation Algorithm. An estimate of the expectile $\hat{\xi}_{\nu}$ is formed from a sample path of length $n$ of the underlying Markov chain. More precisely, let $H_{n}=(s_{0},C_{0},\dots,s_{n-1},C_{n-1})$, where $s_{i}\in\mathcal{S}$ are the states visited, and $C_{i}$ the corresponding cost sample. Thus, an expectile estimator is a map $\hat{\xi}_{\nu,n}:H_{n}\to\mathbb{R}$.
L163: We first provide a lower bound that characterizes the fundamental limit on the expectile estimation problem in a Markovian framework.
L164: ###### Theorem 2.
L165: 
L166: For an MCP $(M,C)\in\mathcal{H}$, Let $\xi_{\nu}(M,C)$ be the expectile of an MCP $(M,C)\in\mathcal{H}$. Then, for every sample path length $n\in\mathbb{N}$ and for any algorithm $\mathscr{A}$ that outputs an expectile estimate $\hat{\xi}_{\nu,n}$, we have
L167:  | $\displaystyle\inf_{\mathscr{A}}\sup_{\begin{subarray}{c}(M,C)\\
L168: \in\mathcal{H}\end{subarray}}\mathbb{P}\left(\left|\hat{\xi}_{\nu,n}-\xi_{\nu}(M,C)\right|\geq\frac{\ln{\left(\frac{1}{2\delta}\right)}}{2\sqrt{n}}\right)\geq\delta,$  |  | (12)
L169: 
L170: for any $\delta\in\left[\frac{1}{2e},\frac{1}{2}\right)$.
L171: 
L172: In addition,
L173: 
L174:  | $$\inf_{\mathscr{A}}\sup_{(M,C)\in\mathcal{H}}\mathbb{E}\left[\left|\hat{\xi}_{\nu,n}-\xi_{\nu}(M,C)\right|\right]\geq\frac{3}{32\sqrt{n}}.$$  |  | (13)
L175: We estimate the expectile of the cumulative discounted cost $Z$ for a discounted Markov chain with a finite horizon $T$. More precisely, given a budget of $n$-transitions, we have $m=\left\lceil\frac{n}{T}\right\rceil$ trajectories. Let $Z_{1},\dots,Z_{m}$ denote the cumulative discounted cost samples obtained from the $m$ trajectories over the finite horizon $T$. With these samples, we form an estimate $\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}$ of expectile of the cumulative discounted cost random variable $Z$ with (cite83†8 ).
L176: ###### Theorem 3.
L177: 
L178: Assume the cumulative discounted cost $Z$ has a finite second moment. Let $\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}$ denote expectile estimator defined by(cite83†8 ) using sample path of $n$ transitions and horizon length $T$. Let $m=\left\lceil\frac{n}{T}\right\rceil$. Then, we have
L179: 
L180:  | $$\mathbb{E}|\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}-\xi_{\nu}(Z)|\leq\frac{L_{\nu}}{\sqrt{m}\mu_{\nu}}\sqrt{\mathbb{E}[(Z-\xi_{\nu})^{2}]}.$$  |  | (14)
L181: 
L182: In addition, if $Z$ is $\sigma$-sub Gaussian, then
L183:  | $$\mathbb{P}[|\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}-\xi_{\nu}(Z)|>\epsilon]\leq 2\exp{\left(-\frac{m\mu_{\nu}^{2}\epsilon^{2}}{8L_{\nu}^{2}\sigma^{2}}\right)},$$  |  | (15)
L184: 
L185: where $\mu_{\nu},L_{\nu}$ are given in (cite84†6 ).
L186: ###### Remark 1.
L187: 
L188: For all $t=0,\dots,T-1$, if $\mathbb{E}[C_{t}^{2}]\leq K<\infty$, it follows that $Z$ has a finite second moment; furthermore, if $C_{t}$ is sub-Gaussian, then $Z$ is also sub-Gaussian.
L189: 
L190: ###### Remark 2.
L191: 
L192: Comparing (cite86†14 ) with the lower bound in (cite87†13 ) shows that the expectation bound exhibits the same dependence on the sample path length $n$.
L193: ###### Remark 3.
L194: Inverting the tail bound in (cite88†15 ) yields the following high-confidence estimate: for any $\delta\in(0,1)$, $|\hat{\xi}_{\nu}^{m}-\xi_{\nu}(Z)|\leq\sqrt{\frac{8L_{\nu}^{2}\sigma^{2}}{m\mu_{\nu}^{2}}\ln{\left(\frac{2}{\delta}\right)}}$ holds with probability $1-\delta$. A comparison with the lower bound in (cite89†12 ) confirms that both bounds share the same dependence on the sample path length $n$.
L195: However, there is a discrepancy regarding the confidence parameter $\delta$; specifically, the lower bound scales with $\log(1/\delta)$, whereas the upper bound scales with $\sqrt{\log(1/\delta)}$.
L196: ### 4.2 Policy gradient for expectiles
L197: 
L198: Our primary objective is to identify the policy parameters $\theta^{*}_{\text{exp}}\in\Theta$ that minimize the expectile risk associated with the return distribution induced by the policy $\pi_{\theta}$. Specifically, for a given asymmetry coefficient $\nu\in(0,1)$, we define the expectile risk $\xi_{\nu}:\Theta\to\mathbb{R}$ as follows:
